  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 149 PM EST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nRest of Today-Tonight...Earlier showers along southern portions of\nthe Treasure Coast continue to drift south of the area this\nafternoon, as the earlier, weaker front settles over the southern\npeninsula. Deepening low pressure over the Mid-Atlantic will drift\nNE through tonight, dragging a reinforcing, stronger cold front\nthrough the local area. Once the existing showers clear the\nforecast area later this afternoon, mostly dry conditions are \nexpected overnight. Can't rule out a stray shower along the front \ntowards morning, mainly over the interior, but PoPs remain 15% or \nless. Will start to see cooler air advect from the north along NNW\nflow behind the front, with lows falling into the mid to upper \n50s from Orlando northward by daybreak. \n\nFriday-Saturday...The low slowly drifts off of the Northeast US\ncoast, as ridging drifts eastward over the central states.\nLocally, this will maintain NNW/NNE flow through Saturday. Winds\n10-15 mph in the afternoon could gust up to 20 mph at times. PoPs\nless than 10% through the period, under clearing skies. Much\ncooler, drier air will be the main story. Highs will remain in the\nlower to mid-70s on Friday, beginning the below normal trend.\nHowever, they will struggle to hit 70 on Saturday, with forecast\ntemperatures peaking in the 60s (10-15 degrees below normal). The\ncoolest night will be Friday night, as lows dip into the mid to\nupper 40s. Coastal areas south of Cape Canaveral look to remain in\nthe lower 50s. Winds remaining near 5-10 mph overnight will\nproduce wind chills in the lower to mid-40s. By Saturday night,\nwinds begin to veer NE over the Atlantic waters, keeping \ntemperatures in the upper 40s to lower 50s. Wind chills in the\nlower 40s will remain possible north and west of I-4.\n\n\nSunday-Thursday...Strong ridging builds eastward across the US,\nnorth of the local area, from Sunday into early next week. Beyond\nthat, the forecast remains highly uncertain, with little\npersistence from run to run nor consensus within the models on\ntiming or development of features. Overall, higher confidence in\nonshore flow redeveloping over the Florida peninsula, perhaps\nbecoming breezy to windy. This will help to warm temperatures to\nnearer to normal, in the mid to upper 70s for highs and 60s for\nlows. Moisture also looks to increase, with PoPs returning to the\nforecast. Will need to monitor the threat for minor coastal \nimpacts and locally heavy rainfall; However, it is too early for \nspecifics like timing and placement.",
